### What is the noisy-neighbor problem in a multi-tenant Kafka cluster?

When multiple teams share one Kafka cluster, all traffic competes for the same broker CPU, disk I/O, network bandwidth, and page cache. If one team suddenly spikes traffic, for example Corporate running a month-end batch at National Bank of India, it can saturate broker resources and degrade every other team's produce and consume latency. This is the noisy-neighbor problem, and solving it requires layered isolation.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

/**
 * Corporate team spike: high-throughput batch producer.
 * Without quotas this can starve Retail and Cards.
 */
public class CorporateBatchProducer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.CLIENT_ID_CONFIG, "corp-payments");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BATCH_SIZE_CONFIG, 65536);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.LINGER_MS_CONFIG, 10);

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
                producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("corp.payments", "TXN-" + i, "{\"amount\":50000}"));
            }
            System.out.println("Sent 1M corporate payment events");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Sent 1M corporate payment events
```

### How do Kafka quotas throttle a noisy tenant?

Kafka quotas are the key runtime tool. You set a produce byte-rate and a consume byte-rate limit per client-id or per authenticated user. When a tenant exceeds its quota, the broker delays that tenant's requests rather than rejecting or dropping them, so the tenant slows down but its messages are not lost and other tenants keep their fair share.

```bash
# Throttle the 'corp-payments' client to 10 MB/s produce and 20 MB/s consume
kafka-configs.sh --alter --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 \
  --add-config 'producer_byte_rate=10485760,consumer_byte_rate=20971520' \
  --entity-type clients --entity-name corp-payments

# Also cap request-handler thread usage to 25%
kafka-configs.sh --alter --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 \
  --add-config 'request_percentage=25' \
  --entity-type clients --entity-name corp-payments

# Verify the quota is applied
kafka-configs.sh --describe --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 \
  --entity-type clients --entity-name corp-payments
```

```text
Configs for client-id 'corp-payments' are:
  producer_byte_rate=10485760
  consumer_byte_rate=20971520
  request_percentage=25
```

### How does topic-level isolation work?

Tenants should not share a single topic. Each team gets its own topics. Sharing a topic couples tenants in partition contention, retention, access control, and schema evolution. Separate topics let you set per-topic configuration and ACLs independently.

```bash
# Retail team topics
kafka-topics.sh --create --topic retail.transfers \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --partitions 12 --replication-factor 3

# Corporate team topics
kafka-topics.sh --create --topic corp.payments \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --partitions 24 --replication-factor 3

# Cards team topics
kafka-topics.sh --create --topic cards.settlements \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --partitions 12 --replication-factor 3

# Each topic has independent retention
kafka-configs.sh --alter --topic retail.transfers \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --add-config retention.ms=604800000   # 7 days
kafka-configs.sh --alter --topic corp.payments \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --add-config retention.ms=2592000000  # 30 days
```

### How does broker-level isolation pin critical workloads?

Using replica placement, you can pin a tenant's topic partitions to a dedicated set of brokers. This gives physical resource separation inside one logical cluster for tenants that need more than quotas but not a whole cluster.

```bash
# Assign cards.settlements partitions to brokers 4, 5, 6 (dedicated Cards group)
kafka-reassign-partitions.sh --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 \
  --reassignment-json-file cards-reassignment.json --execute

# cards-reassignment.json:
# { "partitions": [
#     {"topic": "cards.settlements", "partition": 0, "replicas": [4,5,6]},
#     {"topic": "cards.settlements", "partition": 1, "replicas": [5,6,4]},
#     {"topic": "cards.settlements", "partition": 2, "replicas": [6,4,5]}
# ] }
```

### How does consumer group isolation prevent blocking?

Each tenant uses its own consumer group with its own committed offsets. Because offsets are per group, a slow or stuck consumer in one group only grows that group's lag and never blocks another group from reading the same or different topics.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Properties;

public class IsolatedConsumerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "retail-settlement-group"); // Retail's own group
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(List.of("retail.transfers"));
            while (true) {
                ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
                records.forEach(r -> System.out.println("Retail: " + r.key() + " -> " + r.value()));
            }
        }
        // Corporate's slow consumer in 'corp-settlement-group' does not affect this group
    }
}
```

```text
Retail: TXN-101 -> {"amount":25000}
Retail: TXN-102 -> {"amount":15000}
```

### How does hard cluster isolation work for critical workloads?

If a workload is business-critical, you give it a physically separate Kafka cluster. This is the most expensive option in hardware and operations, so you reserve it for the few workloads where any cross-tenant impact is unacceptable. At National Bank of India, the Cards settlement cluster is completely separate so no other team's spike can ever touch it.

```bash
# Cards settlement runs on its own cluster (separate bootstrap servers)
# bootstrap.servers=cards-kafka-1:9092,cards-kafka-2:9092,cards-kafka-3:9092

# Shared cluster for Retail and Corporate (with quotas)
# bootstrap.servers=shared-kafka-1:9092,shared-kafka-2:9092,shared-kafka-3:9092
```

### What are common mistakes in multi-tenant Kafka?

The most common mistake is onboarding a new team with no quotas, no separate topics, and unbounded shared defaults, so the first traffic spike creates a noisy-neighbor incident.

```bash
# WRONG: new team uses the shared 'transactions' topic with no quotas
# All teams compete for the same partitions and resources

# CORRECT: tenant onboarding checklist
# 1. Create team-specific topics with per-topic retention
kafka-topics.sh --create --topic newteam.events \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3
# 2. Set per-client quotas before the first message
kafka-configs.sh --alter --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 \
  --add-config 'producer_byte_rate=5242880,consumer_byte_rate=10485760' \
  --entity-type clients --entity-name newteam-app
# 3. Set ACLs so the team cannot write to other teams' topics
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

These layers are complementary. Quotas plus per-team topics plus per-group consumers are the cheap default that handles most noisy-neighbor cases. Broker-group isolation is the middle tier. A separate cluster is the top tier. Add per-tenant observability tracking per-client byte rates, request-handler utilization, and per-group lag, so you detect a noisy neighbor quickly and tighten its quota before it hurts anyone.

```bash
# Per-client monitoring
# JMX: kafka.server:type=FetcherLagMetrics,name=ConsumerLag,clientId=corp-payments
# JMX: kafka.server:type=Produce,name=ByteRate,clientId=corp-payments
# Grafana dashboard: per-client-id produce rate, consume rate, throttle time
```
